Concatenates the two items at the top of the stack (that is, puts them end to end). This opcode has been disabled, so it is currently impossible to use. However, it has recently come back into the spotlight. Some would like to add it to Tapscript to allow items on the stack to be combined, and thus improve the expressiveness of this language. This simple additional tool could allow:
* The use of Lamport signatures, which are believed to be resistant to quantum attacks;
* The implementation of vaults;
* The use of covenants;
* Or the use of non-equivocation contracts.

Makes the transaction invalid unless all of these conditions are met:
* The stack is not empty;
* The value at the top of the stack is greater than or equal to `0`;
* The timelock type is the same between the `nLockTime` field and the value at the top of the stack (real time or block number);
* The input's `nSequence` field is not equal to `0xffffffff`;
* The value of the transaction's `nLockTime` field is greater than or equal to the value at the top of the stack.

If any one of these conditions is not met, the script containing the `OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY` cannot be satisfied. If all these conditions are valid, then `OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY` acts like a `OP_NOP`, that is, it performs no action on the script. It is a bit as if it disappeared. `OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY` therefore imposes a time constraint on spending the UTXO secured with the script containing it. It can do so either in the form of a date expressed in Unix time, or in the form of a block number. To do this, it restricts the possible values for the `nLockTime` field of the transaction that spends it, and this `nLockTime` field itself restricts the moment at which the transaction can be included in a block.

*This opcode is a replacement for `OP_NOP`. It was placed on the `OP_NOP2`. It is often referred to by its acronym “CLTV”. Note that `OP_CLTV` must not be confused with the `nLockTime` field of a transaction. The former uses the latter, but their natures and actions are different.*

Makes the transaction invalid if any one of these characteristics is observed:
* The stack is empty;
* The value at the top of the stack is less than `0`;
* The disable flag of the value at the top of the stack is not set and; The transaction version is less than `2` or; The disable flag of the input's `nSequence` field is set or; The timelock type is not the same between the `nSequence` field and the value at the top of the stack (real time or number of blocks) or; The value at the top of the stack is greater than the value of the input's `nSequence` field.

If one or more of these characteristics is observed, the script containing the `OP_CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY` cannot be satisfied. If all the conditions are valid, then `OP_CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY` acts like a `OP_NOP`, that is, it performs no action on the script. It is a bit as if it disappeared. `OP_CHECKSEQUENCEVERIFY` therefore imposes a relative time constraint on spending the UTXO secured with the script containing it. It can do so either in the form of a real time, or in the form of a number of blocks. To do this, it restricts the possible values for the `nSequence` field of the input that spends it, and this `nSequence` field itself restricts the moment at which the transaction that includes this input can be included in a block.

*This opcode is a replacement for `OP_NOP`. It was placed on the `OP_NOP3`. It is often referred to by its acronym “CSV”. Note that `OP_CSV` must not be confused with the `nSequence` field of a transaction. The former uses the latter, but their natures and actions are different.*

In hierarchical deterministic (HD) wallets, the purpose, defined by BIP-0043, represents a specific derivation level. This index, located at the first depth of the derivation tree (`m / purpose' /`), identifies the derivation standard adopted by the wallet, in order to facilitate compatibility between different wallet management software. For example, in the case of SegWit addresses (BIP-0084), the purpose is denoted `/84'/`. This method makes it possible to organize keys efficiently between the different address types within a single HD wallet. The standard indexes used are:
* For P2PKH: `44'`;
* For P2WPKH-nested-in-P2SH: `49'`;
* For P2WPKH: `84'`;
* For P2TR: `86'`.

![BIP-84 tree with an arrow pointing to the Purpose level (m/84'), which identifies the derivation standard followed.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/o/objectif/assets/image-1.png)

Refers to a block with no child: a valid block, but one excluded from the main Bitcoin chain. It occurs when two miners find a valid block at the same chain height within a short period of time and broadcast it on the network. Nodes eventually choose a single block to include in the chain, according to the principle of the chain with the most accumulated work, rendering the other “stale”. The process leading to the production of a stale block is as follows:
* Two miners find a valid block at the same chain height within a short time interval. Let's call them `Bloc A` and `Bloc B`;
* Each broadcasts its block to the network of Bitcoin nodes. Each node now has 2 blocks at the same height. There are therefore two valid chains;
* The miners continue searching for proofs of work for the next blocks, but to do so, they must choose a single block, between `Bloc A` and `Bloc B`, on top of which they will mine;
* A miner finally finds a valid block on top of `Bloc B`. Let's call it `Bloc B+1`;
* It broadcasts `Bloc B+1` to the nodes of the network;
* Since nodes follow the longest chain (the one with the most accumulated work), they will consider that `Chaîne B` is the one to follow;
* They will abandon `Bloc A`, which is no longer part of the main chain. It has therefore become a stale block.

![Temporary fork at height 145: block 145-A becomes stale while 145-B is extended by block 146.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/o/obsolete-bloc/assets/image-1.png)

*In French, one can also say “bloc périmé” or “bloc abandonné”. Even though I do not agree with this usage, some bitcoiners use the term “orphan block” to refer to what is in reality a stale block.*
